$\begin{align}
3y&=\cos(x)
\\\\
3\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dt}&=-\sin(x)\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$

We are given that $\dfrac{dy}{dt}=5$, and we want to find $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ when $x=\dfrac{\pi}{2}$.

Let's plug $\blueD{x=\dfrac{\pi}{2}}$ and $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}=5}$ into the equation we obtained:

$\begin{align}
3\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}}&=-\sin(\blueD{x})\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
3\cdot\goldD{5}&=-\sin\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}\right)\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
15&=-1\cdot \dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
-15&=\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$
